+# The chaos matrix
+
+A fault is only understood when three questions are answered about it: what is injected, what a
+client sees, and what the cluster must still hold. This page is the project's answer for every fault
+it can inject — and the code holds the same table, so a row that changes here has to change there
+(the spec compares the two).
+
+## The matrix
+
+| fault | client sees | invariant |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| partition | writes sent to the isolated side are refused; the majority side keeps committing | a committed write stays committed on every node once the link heals |
+| crash | writes sent to the crashed node are refused until it restarts | the crashed node catches up from the log when it comes back |
+| drop | the dropped messages are lost; the client retries with the same idempotency key | the retry is not a second write |
+| restart | the node is unavailable for the length of the restart, then serves again | a rolling restart never leaves fewer than a majority up |
+
+## How to read a row
+
+The **client sees** column is the sentence a caller can act on: refused means try the leader (or wait),
+served means the answer is real. The **invariant** column is the sentence a test asserts: it is what
+must be true of the cluster after the fault, whatever the schedule was.
+
+The two columns are deliberately different in kind. "Writes are refused while the node is down" is
+observable by a caller and useless as an assertion after the fact; "the node catches up when it comes
+back" is the thing a drill checks. A table that put the same sentence in both columns would be a table
+with one column.
+
+## Adding a fault
+
+A fault outside this matrix has no expected behaviour, which makes injecting it an experiment rather
+than a drill. Adding one means adding a row here **and** a case in `src/chaos.rs` — the spec refuses a
+matrix that is not the same on both sides, which is the only way this page stays true.
